.SH "ddc_server.opt FILE SYNTAX"
.IX Header "ddc_server.opt FILE SYNTAX"
This manpage describes the syntax of \fIddc_server.opt\fR
server option files (\*(L"server-opt files\*(R") used by the \s-1DDC\s0 corpus indexing system.
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 2
\& ##\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
\& ## Option Processor Directives
\& 
\& #comment
\& //comment
\& include "OPTFILE"
\&
\& ##\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
\& ## Logging Options
\& 
\& LogLevel LEVEL
\& SlowQueryTimeout SECONDS
\& StatsWindow SECONDS
\&
\& ##\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
\& ## Client\-Thread Options
\& 
\& NumThreads NTHREADS
\& ThreadQueueSize QSIZE
.Ve
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
This section contains descriptions of the various options and flags
which may occur in a \s-1DDC\s0 server opt-file as read by the
\&\f(CW\*(C`ddc_daemon\*(C'\fR program versions v2.1.0 and later.
.PP
Note that corpus option-files (\fI\s-1CORPUS\s0.opt\fR) are documented independently;
see \fIddc_opt\fR\|(5) for details.
.SS "Option Processor Directives"
.IX Subsection "Option Processor Directives"
\fI#comment\fR
.IX Subsection "#comment"
.PP
\fI//comment\fR
.IX Subsection "//comment"
.PP
Comments are lines starting with a literal hash-mark (\f(CW\*(C`#\*(C'\fR) or two adjacent slashes (\f(CW\*(C`//\*(C'\fR).
Such lines are ignored by the option processor.
.PP
\fIinclude\fR
.IX Subsection "include"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& include "OPTFILE"
.Ve
.PP
The \f(CW\*(C`include\*(C'\fR directive reads in the contents of an external server-opt file at the
current position.  If \s-1OPTFILE\s0 is a relative pathname, it is interpreted relative
to the directory containing the file currently being parsed.
.SS "LoggingOptions"
.IX Subsection "LoggingOptions"
The following options control the verbosity of server log messages.
.PP
\fILogLevel\fR
.IX Subsection "LogLevel"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& LogLevel LEVEL
.Ve
.PP
Aliases: LogLevel, Log, Verbose
.PP
Set the default maximum log-level for server messages to \fI\s-1LEVEL\s0\fR,
which should be one of the following:
.IP "silent" 4
.IX Item "silent"
Suppress (almost) all log messages.
Aliaes: silent, none, no, off.
.IP "error" 4
.IX Item "error"
Log only error messages.
Aliases: error, errors.
.IP "warning" 4
.IX Item "warning"
Log error and warning messages.
Aliases: warn, warning, warnings.
.IP "info" 4
.IX Item "info"
Log errors, warnings, and basic info messages.
Aliases: info, default, weak, light.
.IP "debug" 4
.IX Item "debug"
Log errors, warning, info, and debugging messages.
Aliases: debug, medium.
.IP "trace" 4
.IX Item "trace"
Log everything we can.
Aliases: trace, heavy.
.PP
\fISlowQueryTimeout\fR
.IX Subsection "SlowQueryTimeout"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& SlowQueryTimeout SECONDS
.Ve
.PP
Aliases: SlowQueryTime
.PP
Default: 10
.PP
Log any requests whose processing requires at least \fI\s-1SECONDS\s0\fR second(s) as \*(L"slow queries\*(R".
\&\fI\s-1SECONDS\s0\fR is parsed as a double-precision float, so non-integral numbers can be specified,
but will only be meaningful if \s-1DDC\s0 was compiled with high-resolution clock support \*(-- in
this case, your \s-1DDC\s0 logs should show nanosecond resolution.
.PP
\fIStatsWindow\fR
.IX Subsection "StatsWindow"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& StatsWindow SECONDS
.Ve
.PP
Aliases: StatWindow, MovingAverageWindow
.PP
Default: 600
.PP
Size of the time window for tracking moving average statistics, in seconds.
Currently affects only the \f(CW\*(C`qtavg\*(C'\fR status property (average query time).
.PP
Since v2.1.1.
.SS "Client-Thread Options"
.IX Subsection "Client-Thread Options"
The following options control the behavior of per-client \*(L"worker\*(R" threads.
.PP
\fINumThreads\fR
.IX Subsection "NumThreads"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& NumThreads NTHREADS
.Ve
.PP
Aliases: NumThreads, NThreads, Threads, NumWorkers, NWorkers, Workers
.PP
Default: 0
.PP
Specifies the number of client worker threads to be used.
If \fI\s-1NTHREADS\s0\fR is zero (the default), no client
worker-threads are created and \f(CW\*(C`ddc_daemon\*(C'\fR runs in pure serial mode,
as for \s-1DDC\s0 < v2.1.0.
Otherwise,
\&\fI\s-1NTHREADS\s0\fR worker threads will be initialized on server startup for
each server node (branch\- or leaf-server), in addition to the
\&\*(L"main\*(R" server threads themselves (which are always allocated),
and \f(CW\*(C`ddc_daemon\*(C'\fR will be started in \*(L"client-thread\*(R" mode.
In this mode, the \*(L"main\*(R" server threads are only responsible
for accepting incoming client connections and enqueueing them
for later processing by a worker thread, so that up to
\&\fI\s-1NTHREADS\s0\fR simultaneous client connections can be handled
in parallel.
.PP
\fIThreadQueueSize\fR
.IX Subsection "ThreadQueueSize"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& ThreadQueueSize QSIZE
.Ve
.PP
Aliases: ThreadQueueSize, QueueSize, QSize
.PP
Default: 256
.PP
Specifies the maximum number of pending client connections in the
work queue in client-thread mode (see \*(L"NumThreads\*(R").
If all worker threads are occupied
and \fI\s-1QSIZE\s0\fR additional client connections have been accepted and
enqueued for processing, then the main accepting server thread will block
until the queue shrinks below \fI\s-1QSIZE\s0\fR before enqueueing any new client
connection \*(-- this basically just pushes the burden of client-queueing
beyond \fI\s-1QSIZE\s0\fR to the \s-1TCP\s0 stack.
